      "message": "## Critical Issues: Memory Safety \u0026 Resource Management\n\n*   **Missing RAII for `libusb` Resources (Memory \u0026 Handle Leaks):**\n    The current implementation manually manages `libusb` initialization, device lists, and device handles (`libusb_get_device_list`, `libusb_open`, etc.). If a function returns early due to an error, `libusb_free_device_list` or `libusb_close` is skipped, causing leaks.\n    *   *C++20 Remediation:* Wrap C-style `libusb` resources in RAII objects. Use `std::unique_ptr` with custom deleters to guarantee cleanup regardless of how the function exits.\n        ```cpp\n        // Example custom deleter for device list\n        struct LibusbDeviceListDeleter {\n            void operator()(libusb_device** list) const {\n                if (list) libusb_free_device_list(list, 1);\n            }\n        };\n        using LibusbDeviceListPtr \u003d std::unique_ptr\u003clibusb_device*, LibusbDeviceListDeleter\u003e;\n        \n        // Usage inside getDeviceCount:\n        libusb_device** list \u003d nullptr;\n        ssize_t count \u003d libusb_get_device_list(nullptr, \u0026list);\n        LibusbDeviceListPtr devs(list); // Automatically freed on return\n        ```\n*   **Buffer Safety and Out-of-Bounds Reads (`std::span`):**\n    Functions like `iapSendCommand`, `getChecksum`, and `sendData` take a raw pointer and a length (`unsigned char *pBuff, int length`). This C-style idiom is prone to size mismatches and over-reads (e.g., in `getChecksum`, if `length` isn\u0027t a multiple of 4, the loop over-reads the buffer).\n    *   *C++20 Remediation:* Replace raw pointer/length pairs with `std::span`. This provides bounds information natively and integrates safely with range-based loops and standard algorithms.\n        ```cpp\n        // Instead of: int getChecksum(unsigned char *pBuff, int length, uint32_t *pChecksum)\n        // Do this:\n        std::optional\u003cuint32_t\u003e getChecksum(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buffer) {\n            if (buffer.size() % 4 !\u003d 0) return std::nullopt; // Safe boundary check\n            uint32_t checksum \u003d 0;\n            for (size_t i \u003d 0; i \u003c buffer.size(); i +\u003d 4) {\n                checksum +\u003d buffer[i] | (buffer[i + 1] \u003c\u003c 8) | \n                            (buffer[i + 2] \u003c\u003c 16) | (buffer[i + 3] \u003c\u003c 24);\n            }\n            return checksum;\n        }\n        ```\n*   **Out-of-Bounds Access in CLI Parsing:**\n    In `ParseCommandLine`, `pArgv[i + 1]` is accessed without checking if `i + 1 \u003c Argc`. \n    *   *C++20 Remediation:* Convert `argv` to a `std::span\u003cconst char*\u003e` or a `std::vector\u003cstd::string_view\u003e` to safely iterate over the arguments and validate boundaries natively.\n\n## Modern C++20 Idioms \u0026 Refactoring\n\n*   **Undefined Behavior / Unaligned Type Punning:**\n    Lines like `*(reinterpret_cast\u003cuint32_t *\u003e(dataSend + 4)) \u003d length;` violate strict aliasing and can cause unaligned access faults on certain architectures.\n    *   *Remediation:* Since you are packing integers into a byte stream to send over USB, explicit bitwise shifts are the safest cross-platform method. If you were copying trivially copyable types of the same size, C++20\u0027s `std::bit_cast` would be appropriate, but manual endian-aware packing is best here to avoid platform-specific endianness bugs.\n*   **Redundant Output Parameters and File I/O:**\n    `getBinarySize` and `getBinary` use out-parameters (`size_t *pLength`, `unsigned char *pBinary`). Furthermore, they manually call `.close()` on `std::fstream`, ignoring the stream\u0027s built-in RAII destructors.\n    *   *C++20 Remediation:* Return the data directly. Use `std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e` to manage the buffer lifetime, and let the `std::fstream` destructor handle file closing automatically.\n        ```cpp\n        std::optional\u003cstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e\u003e readBinary(const std::string\u0026 filePath) {\n            std::ifstream binFile(filePath, std::ios::binary | std::ios::ate);\n            if (!binFile) return std::nullopt;\n            \n            auto size \u003d binFile.tellg();\n            binFile.seekg(0, std::ios::beg);\n            \n            std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e buffer(size);\n            if (binFile.read(reinterpret_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(buffer.data()), size)) {\n                return buffer;\n            }\n            return std::nullopt;\n        }\n        ```\n*   **Type-Safe Formatting (`std::format`):**\n    The codebase relies heavily on C-style `printf` for logging, which is not type-safe and requires rigid format specifiers (e.g., `%ld`, `%02X`).\n    *   *C++20 Remediation:* Replace `printf` with `\u003cformat\u003e` (or `\u003cprint\u003e` if using C++23). It is faster, type-safe, and easier to read.\n        ```cpp\n        // Old\n        printf(\"[IAP] %s - file size is not match, request: %ld, recv: %ld\\n\", funcName, size, length);\n        \n        // C++20\n        std::cout \u003c\u003c std::format(\"[IAP] {} - file size does not match, request: {}, recv: {}\\n\", \n                                 funcName, size, buffer.size());\n        ```\n\n## Code Style \u0026 Deduplication\n\n*   **Eliminate Global State:**\n    `g_CmdLine`, `g_DispNext`, `g_deviceCount`, and `g_devInfo` create tightly coupled, hard-to-test code. \n    *   *Remediation:* Encapsulate the application state, USB context, and configuration into an `IapController` class. Initialize resources in the constructor and tear them down in the destructor (RAII).\n*   **Boilerplate Command Execution:**\n    Functions like `runIap`, `finishIap`, and `softwareReset` share identical setup, execution, and error-handling logic.\n    *   *Remediation:* Write a single generic command execution function that takes an opcode, an optional `std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e` for payload, and returns a `std::expected\u003cvoid, std::string\u003e` (or a boolean/custom error enum if sticking strictly to C++20 without backporting C++23\u0027s `std::expected`). This will cut the file size of `iapcontrol.cc` in half.",
